<TopicPage slug="subjects/education/foundational/the-learning-process">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Chapters 1 and 2 of Seifert &amp; Sutton's <em>Educational Psychology</em> set the
    foundation for the whole field: chapter 1 establishes teaching as a research-informed
    profession — one that must keep reflecting on its own practice — and chapter 2 asks the
    central question, <strong>what actually happens when someone learns?</strong> Two grand
    answers dominate. The <em>behaviourist</em> view treats learning as a change in observable
    behaviour shaped by association and consequence. The <em>cognitive</em> view opens the
    black box: learning is a change in knowledge and mental structures. Every teaching
    decision you will ever make quietly assumes one of them — which is why a course about
    teaching starts with a course about learning.
  </p>
  <p>
    A first definition holds both views together: <strong>learning is a relatively permanent
    change in behaviour or knowledge that results from experience</strong>. The phrase does
    real work. "Relatively permanent" excludes a yawn, a mood, tonight's cramming (which
    evaporates); "change" excludes what the learner could already do; "results from
    experience" excludes maturation — a toddler's walking is development, not learning. Every
    theory below is an attempt to explain the mechanism behind that change, and every method
    of teaching is an attempt to cause it deliberately. That is the connection between the
    chapter's two halves: the profession exists to industrialise something every brain does
    anyway.
  </p>

  <h2>The changing teaching profession</h2>
  <p>
    Chapter 1's portrait of the profession is of a job in motion, and knowing the motion is
    part of joining it. Teaching has changed along four visible axes. <em>Diversity</em>:
    classrooms now serve learners of many languages, cultures, abilities and prior
    knowledges, which converts "teach the class" into "diagnose the learner" — a
    professional skill, not a personality trait. <em>Technology</em>: from chalk to learning
    management systems to AI tutors, tools change what a teacher plans, monitors and
    personally delivers — without changing what learning requires (attention, encoding,
    retrieval, motivation). <em>Accountability</em>: standards, assessment regimes and
    published results give the profession external scoreboards, with real benefits (focus,
    equity of expectations) and real risks (narrowing what counts as learning). And
    <em>research-informed practice</em>: the expectation that a teacher's decisions are
    justified by evidence, not folklore — the assumption the whole textbook stands on.
  </p>
  <p>
    The thread that ties these together in chapter 1 is <strong>reflective practice</strong>.
    Teaching is a profession of constant <strong>decision-making</strong> — what to teach, to
    whom, in what order, and what to do when it fails — and a professional is someone who
    justifies those decisions with evidence rather than habit. Reflection is the loop that
    makes this possible: plan, act, observe the result, revise. Donald Schön called the
    practitioner's in-the-moment version "reflection-in-action" — the experienced teacher
    noticing mid-lesson that the explanation isn't landing and changing course — and its
    after-the-fact version "reflection-on-action". Both are trainable, both are what
    separates the professional from the merely experienced, and both are the practical
    stance behind this textbook's claim that teachers should read research the way
    physicians read trials.
  </p>
  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> good teaching is a personality — some people are "born
  teachers". The research picture says otherwise: teaching practices (clear explanations,
  well-managed classrooms, checking for understanding, spaced and retrieved practice) are
  learnable and measurable, and experience without reflection often just entrenches habits.
  The profession is built on learnable craft, which is exactly why the learning theory in
  this lesson is part of teacher training at all.</p>

  <h2>Behaviourism: association and consequence</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Classical conditioning (Pavlov):</strong> a neutral signal paired repeatedly with something meaningful acquires its power. In classrooms this is the emotional weather — a subject paired with anxiety or with curiosity carries that pairing with it.</li>
    <li><strong>Operant conditioning (Skinner):</strong> behaviour is shaped by its consequences. <em>Positive</em> reinforcement adds something desirable after a behaviour; <em>negative</em> reinforcement removes something unpleasant — and both make the behaviour more likely. Punishment suppresses behaviour but teaches nothing about what to do instead.</li>
    <li><strong>The classroom takeaway:</strong> immediate, consistent consequences and the promise of reinforcement still explain a great deal of habit, routine and classroom management — while telling you little about understanding.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    Worked example of the theory doing real work: a seven-year-old refuses to write. A
    behaviourist analysis asks what happens immediately after the refusal — the answer is
    often that the child escapes the task (negative reinforcement of the refusal: the
    behaviour "works"), gets attention, or both. The intervention redesigns the
    consequences: break the task into steps small enough to complete, reinforce completion
    immediately (praise, a token, a checked box), and refuse to let refusal produce escape —
    the refusal stops "paying" and the writing starts to. This is standard, evidence-based
    classroom practice, built entirely from Skinner's vocabulary. Schedules matter too:
    reinforcement that is <em>intermittent</em> (every so often, not every time) produces
    behaviour more resistant to extinction than constant reward — the mechanic behind both
    healthy practice habits and slot machines.
  </p>
  <p>
    Behaviourism earned its influence by being measurable: define the behaviour, deliver the
    consequence, watch the behaviour change. Its blind spot is equally instructive. A pupil
    can emit the correct response for the wrong reason, and two pupils responding
    identically may hold completely different understandings — differences behaviourism
    cannot even express, because everything unobservable was out of scope. That limitation
    is precisely what drove the cognitive turn.
  </p>

  <h2>Cognitivism: the memory pipeline</h2>
  <Widget no="1" caption="sensory register → working memory → long-term memory — where learning lives or dies" widget="equationStep" config={memoryCfg} />
  <p>
    The pipeline model, the backbone of the cognitive view in chapter 2, converts vague
    advice into design. Each stage imposes its own constraint. <em>Attention</em> is a
    limited resource you must direct — a classroom competing with phones is a battle for the
    sensory register's gate. <em>Working memory</em> is a bottleneck you must respect: it
    holds roughly 7 ± 2 familiar items (Miller, 1956) — fewer when the items are new — so a
    15-step explanation or a slide with five columns overloads it and produces the teacher's
    saddest outcome: something "explained" and instantly gone. The remedy is
    <em>chunking</em> and sequencing: a 10-digit number is 10 strangers, but a phone number
    is one chunk; new material should connect to what is already known, because connection
    is what turns items into chunks. <em>Encoding</em> is what you build with meaning —
    deep, semantic processing (why is this true? where does it connect?) beats shallow
    processing (copy the definition) by wide margins. <em>Retrieval</em> is the skill that
    makes learning stick: every successful recall re-entrenches the memory, which is why
    testing yourself beats re-reading — the <em>testing effect</em>, one of psychology's
    most replicated results.
  </p>
  <p>
    Overarching the pipeline sits <strong>metacognition</strong> — knowing what you know and
    how you learn. Metacognitive learners plan (what do I need to do?), monitor (is this
    making sense? can I explain it?) and evaluate (what worked? what will I change?), and
    the research consensus is that teaching these habits is itself a learning strategy with
    effects as large as any content intervention. The classroom implication is humble and
    profound: a teacher's job includes building the student's supervisor, not just filling
    the student's storehouse.
  </p>

  <h2>The forgetting curve is brutal — and bendable</h2>
  <Widget no="2" caption="Ebbinghaus (1885): retention of learned material over time (1 hour to 744 hours)" widget="plotter" config={ebbinghausPlot} />
  <p>
    In 1885 Hermann Ebbinghaus memorised nonsense syllables and tested himself at intervals.
    Within <strong>20 minutes he had lost roughly 42%</strong> (retention ~58% — the first,
    steepest point, off this chart's scale); an hour later 44% survived; a day later 33%;
    after a month only about 21%. The plotted line is the original data. Its slope is not a
    law of nature but a warning: <em>without consolidation and rehearsal, most of what is
    learned today is gone tomorrow.</em> Two levers flatten it. <strong>Spacing</strong>:
    the same total review time distributed across days beats massed practice (cramming) —
    Ebbinghaus himself found spaced repetitions far more efficient, and a century of
    subsequent research confirms it across materials and ages. <strong>Retrieval
    practice</strong>: recalling material (closed book) is what consolidates it, so quizzes
    are learning events, not just measurement. Add meaningful encoding (the third lever) and
    the forgetting curve of a real classroom can look nothing like Ebbinghaus's — which is
    exactly the point: his nonsense syllables had no meaning, no schema and no review, so
    his curve is the floor, not the ceiling.
  </p>

  <h2>What the two views share — and where constructivism enters</h2>
  <p>
    Behaviourism says: engineer the environment and consequences. Cognitivism says: engineer
    attention, encoding and retrieval. Neither is "wrong" — they answer different questions,
    and the professional stance of chapter 1 is to know which problem you are solving: a
    disruption habit is a consequence problem; a persistent misconception is a knowledge-
    structure problem, and more gold stars will not fix it. A third tradition completes the
    modern picture: <strong>social constructivism</strong> (Vygotsky) argues that learning is
    fundamentally social — we internalise what we first do with help. Its key term, the
    <em>zone of proximal development</em>, is the gap between what a learner can do alone and
    what they can do with support; good teaching lives in that gap (scaffolding: help that
    is gradually removed), and it explains why pairs, discussion and worked examples so often
    outperform solitary practice. Learning, in all three views, is <em>a durable change
    produced by experience</em>; the argument is only over where to look for it — and the
    mature practitioner borrows from all three shelves.
  </p>

  <h2>Going deeper</h2>
  <p>
    The frontier questions make the two chapters' stakes concrete. First, the
    <strong>expertise-reversal problem</strong>: the scaffolding novices need (worked
    examples, reduced choice) actually <em>hurts</em> experts, whose schemas are ready for
    challenge — so the optimal design depends on the learner's prior knowledge, which is why
    "what works" is always conditional and why the reflective practitioner measures rather
    than assumes. Second, the <strong>desirable difficulties</strong> programme (Bjork):
    conditions that slow visible learning during practice — spacing, interleaving related
    topics, varied problems — improve durable retention, while conditions that feel smooth
    and successful (blocked practice, immediate feedback on everything) often evaporate.
    Learners' judgements of their own learning are therefore systematically unreliable — the
    empirical case for metacognitive training. Third, the neuroscience layer: memory
    consolidation happens substantially during sleep and through hippocampal–cortical
    reorganisation over weeks, which gives the spacing effect a physical basis and turns
    "sleep before the test" from advice into mechanism. Ebbinghaus's curve, Miller's seven,
    Vygotsky's zone: three results, three different methods (self-experiment, laboratory
    experiment, pedagogical observation), one converging conclusion — learning is lawful
    enough to be engineered, and the engineering is the profession.
  </p>
  <p>
    A final worked case shows the two chapters — the profession and the process — operating
    as one. Suppose a class consistently fails to retain the last quarter of each unit's
    material. The reflective practitioner resists the reflex answer ("revise harder") and
    diagnoses through the theory. A <em>behaviourist</em> check: is there any consequence
    attached to the early material later on — or does the course structure quietly reward
    cramming, since nothing after the test depends on it? A <em>cognitive</em> check: is the
    final quarter taught when attention is structurally low (end of lesson, end of term),
    encoded without connection to what came before (so it forms isolated, chunkless items),
    and never retrieved after its test (so no consolidation)? A <em>metacognitive</em>
    check: do students' own judgements of mastery mislead them there — do they rate material
    they have merely re-read as learned? Each check yields a different intervention (spaced
    retrieval quizzes that reopen old material; linking each new topic explicitly to the
    previous; brief self-testing before study). The lesson of the case is the lesson of the
    whole chapter: chapter 2's models are the diagnostic instruments, chapter 1's reflective
    stance is the hand that uses them — and the pairing, not either half alone, is what
    "research-informed teaching" means in practice.
  </p>
  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> Seifert &amp; Sutton, Educational Psychology (2nd ed.), ch. 1–2 (Open Textbook Library); Ebbinghaus, Über das Gedächtnis (1885); Miller, "The Magical Number Seven, Plus or Minus Two", Psychological Review (1956); Schön, The Reflective Practitioner (1983); Vygotsky, Mind in Society (1978); Roediger &amp; Karpicke, "Test-Enhanced Learning" (2006).</p>
</TopicPage>
